  "bodyText": "Home\nVisit Us\nExhibitions\nEvents\nOnline Talks\nOxfordshire’s Military Heritage in 50 Objects\nCollections\nBlog\nResearch\nLearning\nShop\nCart\nMy Account\nAbout\nBecome a Friend\nSupport Us\nRoom Hire\n\n01993 810 210\n\nfrontofhouse@sofo.org.uk\n\n  \nHome\nEvents\nExhibitions\nCollections\nFriends’ Membership\nVisit\nShop\nBasket\nMy Account\nOxfordshire’s Military Heritage in 50 Objects\nResearch\nLearning\nOnline Talks\nBlog\nSupport Us\nRoom Hire\nImage Services\nAbout\n13\nJun\nBYDUNCAN PARSONS// COLLECTION, GALLERIES, OXFORD AND BUCKS LIGHT INFANTRY\nOxfordshire D-Day Stories: Lt. Colonel John Granville\nOXFORDSHIRE D-DAY STORIES: LT. COLONEL JOHN GRANVILLE\n\nLt Col. John Granville\n\nJohnny Granville joined the 2nd Battalion of the Regiment as a Second Lieutenant in August 1936, when the Battalion was in India.\n\nHe quickly made his mark as an instructor and as a polo player in the regimental team, as well as a fine shot. Posted home early in 1940 he was appointed Adjutant of 7th. Battalion of the Regiment as it was formed in Aldershot.\n\nAs the Battalion expanded he commanded a company but later moved briefly to the newly-formed Reconnaissance Corps before being called back into the 2nd Battalion of the Regiment which had returned from India and had been converted into an air-landing battalion in the 1st. British Airborne Division. This was a new concept requiring the Battalion to be structured round the Horsa Glider and trained to fly in and to fight on landing behind enemy lines.\n\nOn the evening of D Day Major Johnny Granville landed by glider with his C Company to the west of the Orne River and Canal crossings which had been seized earlier by D Company and joined the fight to hold the bridgehead against enemy counter-attacks. Then the advance to the Seine commenced, and C Company played their part in a series of rearguard actions as the enemy withdrew eastwards.\n\nThe 2nd Battalion was finally withdrawn on the 4th. September 1944 to be rested, but on the 23rd December they were rushed to Belgium to counter the German offensive in the Ardennes. Here Johnny Granville and C Company distinguished themselves in the bitter battle for Bure, when reinforcing the 13th Parachute Battalion who were struggling to hold on against repeated enemy counter-attacks.\n\nOn the Battalion’s return to the United Kingdom, Major Granville was sent on a staff course followed by a sequence of staff appointments in Egypt, the War Office and Malaya. Promoted to Lieutenant Colonel, his last appointment was to the Ministry of Supply in 1954- 1955.\n\nLt. Colonel Granville's medals were donated to the museum during the 80th anniversary of D-Day by his family, and can currently be seen on display in our special exhibition, Oxfordshire to the Orne (18 May - 22 September 2024).\n\n \n\n Tags: 2nd Battalion, 52nd, Airborne, C Company, D-Day, D-Day 80, DDay, exhibition, granville, John Granville, Johnny Granville, Lt. Col. John Granville, Medals, Normandy, Ox and Bucks, Oxf and Bucks, Oxf. & Bucks, Oxf. and Bucks LI, Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire Light Infantry, Oxfordshire D-Day Stories, Oxfordshire Stories, Oxfordshire to the Orne, Oxon and Bucks\n\nPrevious:\n80th Anniversary of D-Day: Oxfordshire to the Orne\nNext:\nOxfordshire D-Day Stories: L/Cpl. Fred Greenhalgh – the first casualty of D-Day?\nRelated posts\nOBJECT OF THE MONTH FEBRUARY 2026: SWEETHEART PIN CUSHION\n\nFebruary’s Object of the Month follows a Valentine’s Day theme! Volunteer Geraldine has selected a First World War pin cushion, shaped like a heart, it has quite a story behind it! Sweetheart Pin Cushion By Geraldine Howell Object photography by Colin Morris   This sweetheart pin cushion was made during the First World War by a member of the Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire Light Infantry. Sweetheart cushions were not unknown before…\n\nOBJECT OF THE MONTH (CHRISTMAS 2025): PRINCESS MARY GIFT TIN\n\nDecember’s Object of the Month had to be a Christmas special! Volunteer Geraldine has selected the iconic First World War gift sent out to troops overseas, researching the boxes themselves and where the men of the Oxfordshire & Buckinghamshire Light Infantry would have been spent their Christmas in 1914. Princess Mary’s Gift Boxes By Geraldine Howell Her Royal Highness The Princess Victoria Alexandra Alice Mary was the only daughter of…\n\nSoldiers of Oxfordshire Museum\nPark Street.\nWoodstock\nOxfordshire OX20 1SN\n\nPhone and Email:\nGeneral Enquiries & Event Tickets: 01993 810 211\nfrontofhouse@sofo.org.uk\n\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms and conditions",
